@flexiarg test-registry/outlive-the-process
@title Warrants And Refusals Both Outlive The Minting Process
@keywords durable-backend, in-memory, refusal-trace, ledger, evidence-store, jvm-lifetime
@audience registry implementers, CLI authors
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-19 (claude-12; defect found via five :no-warrant conformance rows same day; fix codex-2, futon3c 224da745: resolve-options forces the HTTP evidence backend, record-refusals! appends typed :refusal entries)
@references [test-registry/register-the-run test-registry/bind-the-subject apparatus/success-must-not-resemble-failure apparatus/replayable-not-precious]
@see-also hygiene/receipt-then-gate
@why the registry's output IS its persistence: a warrant that dies with the JVM that minted it was never evidence, just a println with a hash in it, and a refusal that leaves no trace makes "the service declined" indistinguishable from "nobody asked". Both failure modes shipped: the validation CLI defaulted to the process-local in-memory store, so chains minted through it 404'd from every later process; and refusals were thrown to the caller's terminal and lost.
@how the durable evidence backend is resolved unconditionally at the entry points — a standalone caller gets the HTTP backend (Agency :7070); the process-local default store is never used for validation warrants; only an explicitly supplied isolated backend (library tests) bypasses this. Symmetrically, every mutating operation wraps in a refusal recorder: a typed refusal appends {:entry/type :refusal, :operation, :reason, :details, :attempted-args, :at} to the operation's own ledger before rethrowing, and readers skip :refusal entries. A failed trace append must not mask the original refusal.
@violation-signature a mint that succeeds in-process and 404s from the next one; a CLI whose backend depends on which JVM happened to load it; a refusal whose only record is stderr; a ledger reader that chokes on :refusal entries.
! conclusion: Both outcomes of every registry operation — the warrant and the typed refusal — land in durable, append-only storage that any later process can read.

  + context: The registry library has an in-memory store for tests and a durable HTTP evidence backend, and CLIs get whichever the code path hands them.

  + IF:
    An entry point mints warrants or refuses operations.

  + HOWEVER:
    The in-memory path works perfectly inside one process, so the defect is invisible to the author and appears only to the NEXT reader — the exact reader warrants exist for.

  + THEN:
    Force the durable backend at entry-point resolution, record refusals in the operation's own ledger, and keep readers tolerant of refusal entries.

  + BECAUSE:
    Evidence is a claim addressed to a future process; storage the future process cannot reach makes it self-addressed.

  + evidence: 2026-09-19 conformance run: five subjects :no-warrant because their chains were minted against the atom-backed store of exited JVMs (EV-uniform-run-record among them; re-mint through the fixed CLI queued); futon3c 224da745.
